.Dd October 29, 2026
.Dt OTEL.CONF 5
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm otel.conf
.Nd system OpenTelemetry configuration
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Pa /etc/otel.conf
.Pa /etc/defaults/otel.conf
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
configures how base system programs export telemetry with the
OpenTelemetry protocol (OTLP).
The base system contains no collector or agent: the programs export
directly to the receiver named here.
It is read through one configuration path of the base exporter library
.Pq Xr oe_config_load 3
by every program exporting with OTLP/HTTP:
.Xr dtrace 1
.Pq Fl x Cm otel=otlp ,
.Xr bsdinstruments 8
.Pq Fl -format Cm otel ,
.Xr hwstat 8 ,
.Xr otelpfd 8
and
.Xr otelauditd 8 ,
and by
.Xr otelconf 8 ,
which validates it.
.Pp
Settings are resolved per key, highest precedence first:
.Bl -enum -offset indent -compact
.It
the program's command line;
.It
the standard
.Ev OTEL_*
environment variables;
.It
.Pa /etc/otel.conf ;
.It
.Pa /etc/defaults/otel.conf ;
.It
built-in defaults.
.El
.Pp
.Pa /etc/defaults/otel.conf
is replaced on upgrade and must not be edited.
Put local settings in
.Pa /etc/otel.conf ;
a key set there replaces the default, and a key of an object
.Pq for example one resource attribute
replaces just that key.
.Pp
Both files use UCL syntax
.Pq see Xr libucl 3
restricted to plain values: macros such as
.Ic .include
and file variables are not processed.
Unknown keys, wrongly typed values and duplicated keys are errors,
so a misspelled key never silently falls back to its default.
.Pp
Starting the out-of-the-box telemetry services is controlled by
.Va otel_enable
in
.Xr rc.conf 5 ;
see
.Sx SERVICES .
.Sh EXPORTER KEYS
.Bl -tag -width "host_id_file"
.It Ic enable Pq boolean, default Dv true
Apply this configuration.
When false, programs ignore the file
.Pq as if it did not exist
and the rc.d OpenTelemetry services refuse to start.
.It Ic endpoint Pq string, default Qq
Base URL of the OTLP/HTTP receiver, for example an OpenTelemetry
Collector.
Signals are sent to
.Pa /v1/metrics ,
.Pa /v1/logs
and
.Pa /v1/traces
below it.
.Pp
The default is empty: nothing in the base system listens for OTLP, so
the configuration names no receiver until one is set.
The rc.d services
.Pq Sx SERVICES ,
.Xr otelpfd 8
and
.Xr otelauditd 8
refuse to start without one
.Po
.Ql otelconf check -t
fails
.Pc .
A program a user runs and asks for OTLP explicitly
.Po
.Xr dtrace 1
.Fl x Cm otel=otlp ,
.Xr bsdinstruments 8
and
.Xr hwstat 8
.Fl -format Cm otel
.Pc
then uses the conventional OTLP/HTTP default of the OpenTelemetry
specification,
.Qq http://localhost:4318 ,
where a collector the user runs listens by default.
.It Ic protocol Pq string, default Qq http/json
OTLP transport.
The base exporter implements only
.Qq http/json ;
.Qq http/protobuf
and
.Qq grpc
are refused.
.It Ic compression Pq string, default Qq gzip
.Qq gzip
or
.Qq none .
.It Ic timeout_ms Pq integer, default 10000
Per-request timeout in milliseconds, 1 to 600000.
.It Ic headers_file Pq path, default Qq
File of extra HTTP headers, typically credentials, for
.Ic endpoint ;
see
.Sx CREDENTIALS FILES .
The headers are sent only when the resolved endpoint is
.Ic endpoint
itself, never to an endpoint given on the command line or in
.Ev OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT .
.It Ic host_id_file Pq path, default Pa /etc/hostid
File holding the host UUID, written at boot by the
.Pa hostid
rc.d script.
Its first line becomes the
.Va host.id
resource attribute unless
.Ic resource
sets it.
A missing file is ignored; a file without a UUID is an error.
.Qq
disables the lookup.
.It Ic resource Pq object
Resource attributes attached to every record, as quoted names and
string values.
Names consist of letters, digits and
.Ql .\& ,
.Ql _ ,
.Ql - ,
.Ql / .
An empty value removes an attribute set by the defaults file.
The defaults set
.Va service.namespace
to
.Qq 5bsd
and
.Va deployment.environment.name
to
.Qq production .
At most 32 attributes are accepted.
.It Ic spool Pq object
Persistent buffering of OTLP batches the collector could not take:
.Bl -tag -width "max_bytes"
.It Ic directory Pq path, default Pa /var/spool/otel
Every program exporting with OTLP/HTTP
.Po
.Xr dtrace 1
.Fl x Cm otel=otlp ,
.Xr bsdinstruments 8
.Fl -format Cm otel ,
.Xr hwstat 8 ,
.Xr otelpfd 8 ,
.Xr otelauditd 8
.Pc
that is not given a spool on its command line spools to
.Pa <directory>/<program>.spool
when it may write this directory (the services, which run as root or
switch to
.Ql _otel ,
and root may; other users export without a spool).
Each spool file has one owner at a time, held with a lock on
.Pa <program>.spool.lock ;
a second instance of the same program exports without a spool.
.It Ic max_bytes Pq integer, default 268435456
Bound on buffered data, 1 MiB to 1 TiB, applied to each spool file and
to a command-line spool given without its own bound; batches beyond it
are dropped and counted.
UCL size suffixes such as
.Qq 256mb
are accepted.
.El
.El
.Sh CREDENTIALS FILES
A credentials file holds one header per line as
.Dl Name: value
Blank lines and lines starting with
.Ql #
are ignored.
Names must be HTTP tokens; values must not contain control characters
other than tab.
.Dv Content-Type ,
.Dv Content-Length ,
.Dv Content-Encoding ,
.Dv Host
and
.Dv Connection
are set by the exporter and are refused.
.Pp
The file must be a regular file
.Pq not a symbolic link
owned by root or by the reading user, with no group or other
permission bits: mode 0600 or 0400.
A file that violates this, or that is configured but missing, makes the
whole configuration fail to load, so telemetry is never sent without
the configured credentials.
.Sh FILE CHECKS
Both configuration files must be regular files, not symbolic links,
owned by root or the reading user, not writable by group or others,
at most 64 KiB, and free of NUL bytes.
A missing file is not an error.
A credentials file is limited to 16 KiB and 32 headers.
.Sh SERVICES
The following
.Xr rc.conf 5
variables start the telemetry services; all default to off.
.Bl -tag -width "otel_auditd_enable"
.It Va otel_enable
Master switch.
Each service variable below that is left empty follows it.
The services need an export target
.Pq Ic endpoint ,
or
.Va otel_endpoint
in
.Xr rc.conf 5 .
.It Va otel_hwstat_enable
.Xr hwstat 8
hardware metrics.
.It Va otelpfd_enable
.Xr pf 4
statistics,
.Xr otelpfd 8 .
.It Va otelauditd_enable
Audit records as log records,
.Xr otelauditd 8 .
.It Va otel_dtrace_enable
DTrace-derived system metrics via
.Xr bsdinstruments 8 .
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width "OTELCONF_DEFAULTS"
.It Ev OTELCONF_FILE , Ev OTELCONF_DEFAULTS
Read these files instead of
.Pa /etc/otel.conf
and
.Pa /etc/defaults/otel.conf ,
for trying a configuration or testing.
Ignored by set-user-ID and set-group-ID programs.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/etc/defaults/otel.conf" -compact
.It Pa /etc/otel.conf
local settings
.It Pa /etc/defaults/otel.conf
default settings
.It Pa /etc/hostid
host UUID
.It Pa /var/spool/otel
spool directory
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Export everything to a remote collector with a bearer token:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# /etc/otel.conf
endpoint = "https://collector.example.org:4318";
headers_file = "/etc/otel/collector.headers";
.Ed
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# install -m 0600 /dev/null /etc/otel/collector.headers
# echo 'Authorization: Bearer TOKEN' > /etc/otel/collector.headers
# otelconf check -t
# sysrc otel_enable=YES
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr dtrace 1 ,
.Xr libotelexport 3 ,
.Xr libucl 3 ,
.Xr oe_sysconf 3 ,
.Xr rc.conf 5 ,
.Xr bsdinstruments 8 ,
.Xr hwstat 8 ,
.Xr otelauditd 8 ,
.Xr otelconf 8 ,
.Xr otelpfd 8
.Sh HISTORY
.Nm
first appeared in 5BSD.
